# NIKATRU — browser extension factory
#
# This file is the ROOT gitignore, and it exists because its absence was a real
# problem rather than an oversight. Until it was added, the only thing keeping a
# live credential out of this public repository was a NESTED .gitignore one
# directory deep (Extension/Full_Screen_Shot/.gitignore). A `.claude/` created
# anywhere else in the tree would have been committed.
#
# The rules below are the specification from the factory's own pipeline document,
# implemented as written rather than reinvented.

# ── secrets: never, under any circumstances ─────────────────────────────
# 🔴 `.claude/` IS BLANKET-IGNORED, AND THAT IS A CHANGE OF POSTURE, NOT A TYPO.
# The factory's own spec argued for narrow rules — "`.claude/` is NOT blanket-ignored;
# hooks, rules, skills and settings must be tracked" — and that trade was made for a
# PRIVATE remote, for tracked material that was subsequently cut. This remote is public,
# and on 2026-08-14 `.claude/` moved from Extension/Full_Screen_Shot/ to the repo ROOT,
# making it the vault the Flutter product's root `.claude/` already is. That repo ignores
# it in full (.gitignore:13) because it holds secrets.env, PEMs, ssh keys and
# service-account JSON.
# A narrow rule protects the ONE secret somebody thought of. A directory rule protects
# the next one nobody has added yet — and in a public repo the difference is permanent.
# ⚠️ Git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT directory is excluded, so no `!` negation
# can carve an exception back out later. That is accepted deliberately: nothing under
# .claude/ needs to be public, and the Flutter product has run this way throughout.
.claude/
**/.claude/
.claude/secrets.env
**/.claude/secrets.env
# A vault file that is NOT inside .claude/. `*.secrets.env` needs a literal `.`
# before the name, so it catches `foo.secrets.env` and misses the obvious one:
# measured, `git check-ignore secrets.env` and `publish/secrets.env` both came
# back NOT ignored, and `git add -A` at the root staged them. The nested
# Extension/Full_Screen_Shot/.gitignore has carried `**/secrets.env` all along —
# the root file, which is the one governing the public repo, was the weaker of
# the two.
**/secrets.env
*.secrets.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.json
# ⚠️ `**/*_PAT*` USED TO STAND HERE AND IT IS A SILENT-DROP HAZARD, not a typo
# fixed. `core.ignorecase` is true on a Windows checkout, so gitignore matching is
# case-INSENSITIVE and `*_PAT*` also matches `_path`, `_patch` and `_pattern`.
# Measured on this tree: `git check-ignore -v core/lib/url_path.js`,
# `scripts/lib/zip_patch.mjs` and `test/net_patterns.js` all named `**/*_PAT*`.
# In a JavaScript factory those are not hypothetical filenames, and the failure is
# the quiet one — `git add -A` drops the file and says nothing, which is exactly
# how templates/tool/HANDOFF.md went missing further down this file. The three
# forms below cover every credential-file shape (`GITHUB_PAT`, `GITHUB_PAT.txt`,
# `MY_PAT_OLD.txt`) while releasing the `_pat…` word family; .githooks/pre-commit
# holds the matching `_PAT($|[^A-Za-z])` and negative-tests all three names.
**/*_PAT
**/*_PAT.*
**/*_PAT_*
# extension signing keys (.pem = the CRX identity)
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.key
*.crx

# ── build output & store packages (they live on Releases, not in git) ───
dist/
build/
out/
web-ext-artifacts/
**/publish/*.xpi
*.xpi
# NOTE: `**/publish/*.zip` is deliberately NOT ignored. Each release zip is a
# golden master — the exact artifact a store received, and what pack.mjs diffs
# the next build against. templates/tool/publish/skeleton-0.0.1.zip is tracked for
# that reason.
#
# 🔴 THE SENTENCE THAT USED TO END THIS BLOCK IS DELETED, BECAUSE IT
# CONTRADICTED THE THREE ABOVE IT. It read: "Real release zips still belong on
# Releases, not here." Beside a rule that deliberately un-ignores them, that
# reads as "keep them out of git", which is the opposite of what the un-ignore
# is for — and it is the sentence that made the disagreement with
# Extension/Full_Screen_Shot/.gitignore look like a matter of taste rather than
# a defect. It was neither. Measured 2026-08-20: the nested file's bare `*.zip`
# won for everything beneath it, so scripts/pack.mjs's dropped-file floor
# graded ZERO entries for the only real tool in this repo, for the whole life
# of the repo, and said so in its own output on every run.
#
# The two purposes are not in competition and are both true:
#   · GITHUB RELEASES is how a zip is DISTRIBUTED — release.yml attaches
#     dist/*.zip to the tag, and that is where anyone else gets one.
#   · publish/*.zip in git is how a zip is COMPARED — pack.mjs floor 3 needs
#     the previous artifact ON DISK to prove no file was silently dropped from
#     the next one. A GitHub Release asset is not on disk at build time.
# Same bytes, two jobs. Doing one does not excuse skipping the other.
#
# ⚠️ Nothing is retroactively committed by this. There are ZERO releases, so
# there is no golden master to anchor yet; the first real release supplies it,
# and pack.mjs keeps printing "graded 0 entries … NOT a clean diff" until then.
# Any *-firefox.zip predating 2026-08-18 must never be tracked OR uploaded —
# see Extension/Full_Screen_Shot/publish/STALE-FIREFOX-ARTIFACTS-2026-08-20.md.

# ── dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.npm/
package-lock.json.bak

# ── generated test output (goldens go in test/**/golden/) ───────────────
**/test/pixel-sim/out/
**/test/e2e/out/
**/test/**/tmp/
coverage/

# ── owner-private material kept OUT of a public repo ────────────────────
# These live in Nikatru_Extensions_Private — a SIBLING directory under Projects/,
# not a child of this one. 📖 The rule for what goes where is now written down at
# docs/PUBLIC-PRIVATE.md; this file only enforces it.
#
# 🔴 THE REPO NAME ABOVE WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-08-20, AND IT IS THE THIRD DEAD NAME
# IN THE STACK. This line read `Nikatru_Chrome_Extensions_Private`, itself a
# correction of `Project_Cross_browser_Extensions_Private`. Both are dead: the
# owner dropped `Chrome` from all four repo names on 2026-08-19, and nothing in
# either tree recorded it. ⚠️ A rename FREES the old name and GitHub keeps
# answering 200 through a redirect until something re-claims it — so a dead name
# in a comment reads as correct right up until the day it silently is not.
# Confirm a repo name with `gh repo list`, NEVER `gh api repos/<owner>/<name>`.
#
# 🔴 `private/` AS A NESTED CLONE IS NOT THE ARRANGEMENT IN EFFECT, and the
# paragraph that used to stand here described one as though it were. There is no
# `private/` directory on disk; the two repositories are siblings. The rule is
# kept — a future clone-in-place must not become committable by accident — but do
# not read it as a description of the layout. `ci.yml` and `scripts/secret-scan.mjs`
# also name `private/`, and are correct for the same defensive reason.
# It MUST stay ignored. Git does not descend into a nested repo, but without this
# line `git add -A` stages the DIRECTORY as a gitlink — and a reader of the public
# repo then sees a commit hash pointing at a repository they cannot fetch, which
# is both a broken reference and an advertisement that the private repo exists.
private/
# competitor UI screenshots — 3rd-party IP
**/Reference/
# competitive strategy
**/MARKET-ANALYSIS.md

# Per-extension planning and handoff documents. Titled "Roadmap & Business Plan",
# "Implementation Plan" and "session handoff" — none of them contains a catalogue
# keyword, which is exactly why a keyword sweep passed them and they were committed
# and pushed on 2026-08-14. THE LESSON IS THE PART TO KEEP: sensitivity lives in a
# document's SUBJECT, not its vocabulary. A sweep that reads words will pass every
# one of these documents every time, because they are written in the same technical
# English as the code they plan.
#
# What happened to the exposure, stated as it now stands rather than as it stood
# when this block was written: the remote was DELETED AND RECREATED EMPTY, so the
# history that carried them is gone rather than merely superseded — measured ON
# THE DAY, `git ls-remote --heads origin` returned nothing at all, and the local
# `origin/main` was a stale cache of a branch that no longer existed anywhere.
# ⚠️ THAT MEASUREMENT IS A DATED RECORD AND IS NO LONGER TRUE OF TODAY: the
# remote was repopulated and now carries branches and history again. The claim is
# kept in the past tense rather than deleted, because it is the evidence that the
# leaked history is gone; it is corrected here because a present-tense "returns
# nothing at all" would be read as a live fact and re-measured to the opposite. The
# earlier wording here said they "reached the public repo … before being removed",
# which described a cleanup commit on top of a live history and is no longer what
# is true. Nothing about the rules below changes: the history was destroyed by an
# owner action that will not be repeated on every future mistake, and these lines
# are the reason there is nothing to destroy next time.
#
# The names are globbed rather than listed one by one, because the leak was a
# document nobody had listed. `*-PLAN` generalises V2-FEATURE-COMPLETE-PLAN.md;
# `*-PROMPT` covers RESUME-PROMPT.md, the agent session-resume note that survived
# the 2026-08-14 cleanup entirely unnoticed — an internal work queue, owner gates
# and session numbering, written in vocabulary so ordinary that no gate looked
# twice. Measured across all 38 markdown files in this tree, the globs below
# select six and no others — the five internal documents above, plus the one
# excepted immediately after them. A legitimate document that does land in them is
# one `git add -f` away, which is the correct direction for the mistake to cost
# something; the reverse mistake costs a leak.
**/ROADMAP.md
**/GIT-SETUP.md
**/*-PLAN.md
**/*-PROMPT.md
**/SESSION-*.md
**/HANDOFF.md
# …with exactly one exception, and it is a PATH, not a word. The skeleton's own
# HANDOFF.md is an unfilled scaffold — `# HANDOFF — ⟨TOOL⟩`, empty bullets, an
# empty session table — and a required template artefact: MIGRATION.md counts it
# among the template's tracked files, templates/tool/TEMPLATE.md's checklist
# requires a stamped tool to fill it in, and templates/tool/README-tour.md names
# it. It is not a session log; it is the blank a session log is written into.
#
# It survived the rule above only by accident: the same commit that added
# `**/HANDOFF.md` also RENAMED this file out of _skeleton/, and git does not
# re-consult .gitignore for a path already in the index. When the history was
# rebuilt from scratch the accident stopped working and `git add -A` silently
# dropped it: measured, templates/tool/ holds 134 files on disk and tracked 133,
# and HANDOFF.md was the entire difference — against the 134 MIGRATION.md's own
# verification step tells a reader to expect. A rule that quietly deletes a
# required file from the product is the same failure as a rule that quietly
# publishes a private one.
!templates/tool/HANDOFF.md
**/*-DELETE-ME.txt
# catalogue, build order, revenue model
TOOLS-PIPELINE.md
# channels, licence-key model
DISTRIBUTION.md
# locked product choices
DECISIONS.md
APP-STACK.md
NAMING.md
I18N-LESSONS.md
# names credential locations
OPERATIONS.md
FULLSHOT-RESEARCH-2026.md
# the operating guide
CLAUDE.md

